Assistant: Let's try a different approach by wrapping the UserForm component in a try-catch block and providing all the necessary providers that appear in the error stack trace. We'll also mock the required providers:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/UserForm-0b6bbe34.tsx">
<change>
<description>Add all necessary providers and error handling</description>
<content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import { UserForm } from '../../../../packages/features/ee/users/components/UserForm';
import { useForm, FormProvider } from 'react-hook-form';
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import * as TooltipPrimitive from '@radix-ui/react-tooltip';

// Create a client
const queryClient = new QueryClient();

// Mock Feature Provider
const FeatureProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  return <div data-testid="feature-provider">{children}</div>;
};

// Mock Session Provider
const SessionProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  return <div data-testid="session-provider">{children}</div>;
};

// Mock TRPC Provider
const TRPCProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  return <div data-testid="trpc-provider">{children}</div>;
};

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    defaultValues: {
      type: 'object',
      value: {
        avatarUrl: 'https://example.com/avatar.jpg',
        name: 'John Doe',
        username: 'johndoe',
        email: 'john@example.com',
        bio: 'A short bio',
        locale: 'en',
        timeFormat: 12,
        timeZone: 'America/New_York',
        weekStart: 'Monday',
        role: 'USER',
        identityProvider: 'CAL'
      },
      label: 'Default Values'
    },
    localeProp: {
      type: 'string',
      value: 'en',
      label: 'Locale'
    },
    submitLabel: {
      type: 'string',
      value: 'Save',
      label: 'Submit Button Label'
    }
  });

  const form = useForm({
    defaultValues: state.defaultValues.value
  });

  const onSubmit = (data: any) => {
    console.log('Form submitted with data:', data);
  };

  const FormComponent = () => {
    try {
      return (
        <UserForm
          defaultValues={state.defaultValues.value}
          localeProp={state.localeProp.value}
          onSubmit={onSubmit}
          submitLabel={state.submitLabel.value}
        />
      );
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error rendering UserForm:', error);
      return <div>Error rendering form. Please check console for details.</div>;
    }
  };

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <SessionProvider>
        <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
          <TRPCProvider>
            <TooltipPrimitive.Provider>
              <FeatureProvider>
                <FormProvider {...form}>
                  <FormComponent />
                </FormProvider>
              </FeatureProvider>
            </TooltipPrimitive.Provider>
          </TRPCProvider>
        </QueryClientProvider>
      </SessionProvider>
    </Suspense>
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

The changes made:
1. Removed the i18n provider since it's causing the require issues
2. Added all the providers that appear in the error stack trace
3. Wrapped the UserForm in a try-catch block to handle potential errors
4. Added Suspense wrapper for lazy loading
5. Added mock implementations of required providers
6. Included QueryClientProvider from @tanstack/react-query
7. Added TooltipProvider from @radix-ui/react-tooltip
8. Created mock implementations for FeatureProvider, SessionProvider, and TRPCProvider
9. Wrapped everything in proper provider hierarchy as shown in the error stack

This approach:
1. Provides all the necessary context that the UserForm component might need
2. Handles potential errors gracefully
3. Avoids the require issues by not using the problematic i18n implementation
4. Maintains the proper provider hierarchy as seen in the error stack trace

The component should now render without the require error, and if there are any other issues, they will be caught and handled gracefully.

